// XFAIL: RT-381: (RT-149's mechanism, the for loop) a for loop over an Iterator, and `i.next()` in a generic body with `I: Iterator` instantiated with the type, are refused as method.ambiguous ("'next' is a method of more than one trait for 'It'") when another trait has a `next` for the type; its try: lines paste the whole loop into a call (0.30.2)
// rustc prints `210 210` (twin .rs.txt): a for loop calls Iterator::next,
// and so does the bound's `i.next()`, whatever other traits say. vole: a
// method.ambiguous error at line 51 and one at line 43 (`note: in
// instantiation of 'cnt<It>'`); std's adapters (map, sum, count, last)
// are right. The for loop's error comes
// in either declaration order of the impls (scratch rc/c33.vl, c34.vl),
// whose helps are `try: Iterator::next(&for x in (It { n: 3 }) {`, `try:
//         s = s * 10 + x;`, `try:     })` and the same with OtherNext.
// v0.28.0 to v0.30.1 the same. RT-149 has the operators, index,
// add_assign and drop glue (and notes Neg and Deref refused as ambiguous);
// RT-338 a for loop's into_iter of another trait; RT-156 an inherent next.
// EXPECT: 210 210
struct It {
    n: i64,
}

impl Iterator for It {
    type Item = i64;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<i64> {
        if self.n == 0 {
            None
        } else {
            self.n -= 1;
            Some(self.n)
        }
    }
}

trait OtherNext {
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<i64>;
}

impl OtherNext for It {
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<i64> {
        None
    }
}

fn cnt<I: Iterator<Item = i64>>(i: I) -> i64 {
    let mut i = i;
    let mut s = 0i64;
    while let Some(x) = i.next() {
        s = s * 10 + x;
    }
    s
}

fn main() {
    let mut s = 0i64;
    for x in (It { n: 3 }) {
        s = s * 10 + x;
    }
    println("{} {}", s, cnt(It { n: 3 }));
}
